  "prostate": {
    "label": "Prostate Cancer Risk",
    "shipped": false,
    "withdrawn_reason": "Held-out test AUC 0.786, 95% CI 0.505 to 0.99. The lower bound sits on chance, so the panel cannot be shown to work. Specificity is 0.571 with a CI of 0.167 to 1.0, an interval carrying no information because the test split is 20 records. It does not beat plain logistic regression (0.769). External validation was searched for and does not exist. The Stanford cohort has no site or centre column, so unlike the pancreatic cohort it cannot be split by institution. NHANES measured serum PSA on 4,697 men across 2005 to 2010, which looked like the answer, but it contains only 17 prostate cancer cases because men with a prostate cancer history are excluded from the PSA subsample. That is far below the roughly 96 events needed. 97 records and two usable features, with no route to an external test, cannot support a clinical claim.",
    "cohort_design": "Case-control and post-prostatectomy. Gleason grade comes from the surgical specimen, not from screening."
  }
